# PSTAT(1M)

## NAME

pstat - print system facts

## SYNOPSIS

| **pstat** [ **-acfimpstx** ] [ **-u** addr ]

## DESCRIPTION

*Pstat* interprets the contents of certain system tables.

**Note:** every table it prints is read by name and by value through kctl(2), which is
unprivileged, so all of it works for anybody - except **-u**, which opens a memory device and
is the super-user's. v7 read every table off */dev/mem* with a namelist taken from */unix*;
there is no kernel image on this filesystem, and the *file* and *namelist* arguments are gone
with it.

**LOC** throughout is the **word** address of the table entry, printed in octal, where v7
printed a 24-bit byte address in hex. So is every **IPTR**, **INODP**, **LINK** and **TEXTP**:
the tables cross-reference by eye.

Options are

**-a**
: Under **-p**, describe all process slots rather than just active ones.

**-i**
: Print the inode table with the these headings:

LOC
: The core location of this table entry.

FLAGS
: Miscellaneous state variables encoded thus:

  > L
  > : locked
  >
  > U
  > : update time filsys(5)) must be corrected
  >
  > A
  > : access time must be corrected
  >
  > M
  > : file system is mounted here
  >
  > W
  > : wanted by another process (L flag is on)
  >
  > T
  > : contains a text file
  >
  > C
  > : changed time must be corrected

CNT
: Number of open file table entries for this inode.

DEV
: Major and minor device number of file system in which this inode resides.

INO
: I-number within the device.

MODE
: Mode bits, see chmod(2).

NLK
: Number of links to this inode.

UID
: User ID of owner.

SIZ/DEV
: Number of bytes in an ordinary file, or major and minor device of special file.

**-x**
: Print the text table with these headings:

LOC
: The core location of this table entry.

FLAGS
: Miscellaneous state variables encoded thus:

  > T
  > : ptrace(2) in effect
  >
  > W
  > : text not yet written on swap device
  >
  > L
  > : loading in progress
  >
  > K
  > : locked
  >
  > w
  > : wanted (L flag is on)

DADDR
: Disk address in swap, measured in multiples of 512 bytes.

CADDR
: Core address, measured in multiples of 64 bytes.

SIZE
: Size of text segment, measured in multiples of 64 bytes.

IPTR
: Core location of corresponding inode.

CNT
: Number of processes using this text segment.

CCNT
: Number of processes in core using this text segment.

**-p**
: Print process table for active processes with these headings:

LOC
: The core location of this table entry.

S
: Run state encoded thus:

  > 0
  > : no process
  >
  > 1
  > : waiting for some event
  >
  > 3
  > : runnable
  >
  > 4
  > : being created
  >
  > 5
  > : being terminated
  >
  > 6
  > : stopped under trace

F
: Miscellaneous state variables, or-ed together, in octal:

  > 01
  > : loaded
  >
  > 02
  > : the scheduler process
  >
  > 04
  > : locked
  >
  > 010
  > : swapped out
  >
  > 020
  > : traced
  >
  > 040
  > : used in tracing
  >
  > 0100
  > : locked in by lock(2).

PRI
: Scheduling priority, see nice(2).

SIGNAL
: Signals received (signals 1-16 coded in bits 0-15),

UID
: Real user ID.

TIM
: Time resident in seconds; times over 127 coded as 127.

CPU
: Weighted integral of CPU time, for scheduler.

NI
: Nice level, see nice(2).

PGRP
: Process number of root of process group (the opener of the controlling terminal).

PID
: The process ID number.

PPID
: The process ID of parent process.

ADDR
: If in core, the physical **word** address of the process image, whose first **USIZE** words are
  its `u-area`. If swapped out, its block on the paging store. **Note:** v7 counted 64-byte
  clicks and 512-byte blocks.

SIZE
: Size of the process image in **words**, a multiple of **PGSZ**.

WCHAN
: Wait channel number of a waiting process.

LINK
: Link pointer in list of runnable processes.

TEXTP
: If text is pure, pointer to location of text table entry.

CLKT
: Countdown for alarm(2) measured in seconds.

**-t**
: Print the table for the **NSC** Consul typewriters, with these headings. **Note:** v7's handled
  a DL11 and up to 48 DH11 lines; this machine has one terminal driver and one array, **sc[]**,
  and the row number is its subscript, which is also the device's minor number.

RAW
: Number of characters in raw input queue.

CAN
: Number of characters in canonicalized input queue.

OUT
: Number of characters in putput queue.

FLAGS
: See tty(4). **Note:** v7 called this column MODE.

LINE
: The device/line number. **Note:** v7 called this ADDR and it was the UNIBUS address of the
  line's registers. This machine has no I/O address space at all - a device is named by the
  register number an **033** instruction addresses - so **t_addr** is a line number here and the
  column is renamed to say so.

DEL
: Number of delimiters (newlines) in canonicalized input queue.

COL
: Calculated column position of terminal.

STATE
: Miscellaneous state variables encoded thus:

  > T
  > : timeout pending. **Note:** nothing sets this bit now; it is kept for v7's numbering and for
  >   this program.
  >
  > W
  > : waiting for open to complete
  >
  > O
  > : open
  >
  > C
  > : carrier is on
  >
  > B
  > : busy doing output
  >
  > A
  > : process is awaiting output
  >
  > X
  > : open for exclusive use
  >
  > H
  > : hangup on close

  **Note: XCLUDE** and **HUPCLS** are set by ioctl(2) and tested by nothing in this kernel, so
  those two letters record a request rather than a state.

PGRP
: Process group for which this is controlling terminal.

**-u**
: Print information about a user process; the next argument is its address as given by ps(1), in
  octal, and with no conversion - both are word addresses. An address of **0** names the u-area
  that is live at **UBASE**, which is the caller's own. **Note:** if the address given is the one
  the live u-area belongs to, the live copy is read instead of the process's image, the image
  being stale until the kernel next flushes it. ps(1) follows the same rule and its source is the
  account of it. The process must be in main memory: a swapped-out u-area is on the paging store
  and this program does not read it. This is the only option that opens a device, and so the only
  one that needs to be root.

**-f**
: Print the open file table with these headings:

LOC
: The core location of this table entry.

FLG
: Miscellaneous state variables encoded thus:

  > R
  > : open for reading
  >
  > W
  > : open for writing
  >
  > P
  > : pipe

CNT
: Number of processes that know this open file.

INO
: The location of the inode table entry for this file.

OFFS
: The file offset, see lseek(2).

**-m**
: **Note: this option is new.** Print the mount table - one line per mounted file system - with
  the headings LOC, DEVICE, BUFP (the buffer holding its super-block) and INODP (the inode it is
  mounted on). Slot 0 is the root and is always in use. With **-a** the empty slots are printed
  too.

**-s**
: **Note: this option is new.** Print the paging store and the two allocation maps: the swap
  device and its **swplo** and **nswap**, then **coremap** and **swapmap**, each with its free
  fragments and their total. **The units differ: coremap** hands out **words** of physical core
  and **swapmap** hands out **blocks** of **BSIZE**.

**-c**
: **Note: this option is new.** Print the system clock: **time** in seconds since the epoch, and
  **lbolt** as a fraction of **HZ** into the current second, followed by the date. time(2)
  returns whole seconds, so the fraction is visible nowhere else.

**Note:** the three options above exist because the kernel exports the tables behind them and
nothing else read them. A variable in **kernel/ksym.c**'s table names the program that asked
for it, and **mount**, **coremap**, **swapmap**, **nswap**, **swplo**, **swapdev**, **time**
and **lbolt** all named this one.

## FILES

/dev/kmem
: the live u-area, for **-u**

/dev/mem
: any other resident u-area, for **-u**

Everything else comes through kctl(2) and opens nothing.

## SEE ALSO

| kctl(2), ps(1), iostat(1M), dmesg(1), stat(2), filsys(5)
| K. Thompson, *UNIX Implementation*
